{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 0,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "doc_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.328,
            0.213,
            0.667,
            0.24
          ],
          "level": 1,
          "content": "6.891 Final Examination"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.455,
            0.296,
            0.539,
            0.319
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "5/23/01"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.178,
            0.361,
            0.442,
            0.384
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "1 Gaussian Units"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.399,
            0.819,
            0.473
          ],
          "content": "Consider a different kind of computing unit for a neural network. Rather than a sigmoid function, it will use a Gaussian. That is, given an input vector x (augmented with a 1 to add the threshold) and a weight vector w the output of the unit will be:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.444,
            0.49,
            0.55,
            0.51
          ],
          "content": "o = g (x \\cdot w)",
          "image_source": "images/page_0_equation_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.205,
            0.524,
            0.263,
            0.541
          ],
          "content": "where"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.439,
            0.553,
            0.556,
            0.575
          ],
          "content": "g (z) = e ^ {- z ^ {2}}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_0_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.201,
            0.591,
            0.818,
            0.634
          ],
          "content": "1. Derive a gradient-descent training rule for a single Gaussian unit (remember that $\\begin{array} { r } { \\frac { d ( e ^ { f ( x ) } ) } { d x } = e ^ { f ( x ) } \\frac { d ( f ( x ) ) } { d x } ) } \\end{array}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.493,
            0.874,
            0.504,
            0.888
          ],
          "content": "1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_idx": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.199,
            0.161,
            0.819,
            0.22
          ],
          "content": "2. Assume that we assign any x for which the output of the unit is greater than or equal to $\\frac { g ( 0 ) } { 2 }$ to class 1 and for which the output is less than $\\frac { g ( 0 ) } { 2 }$ to class 2. What kind of separator do we get?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.177,
            0.337,
            0.492,
            0.359
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "2 Network Structure"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.375,
            0.818,
            0.393
          ],
          "content": "Show a Bayesian network structure that encodes the following relationships:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.205,
            0.409,
            0.416,
            0.426
          ],
          "content": "• A is independent of B"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.206,
            0.44,
            0.475,
            0.458
          ],
          "content": "• A is dependent on B given C"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.206,
            0.47,
            0.406,
            0.488
          ],
          "content": "• A is dependent on D"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.206,
            0.501,
            0.491,
            0.52
          ],
          "content": "• A is independent of D given C."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.492,
            0.874,
            0.505,
            0.888
          ],
          "content": "2"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_idx": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.177,
            0.155,
            0.422,
            0.178
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "3 At The Races"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.194,
            0.469,
            0.212
          ],
          "content": "You go to the racetrack. You can:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.205,
            0.225,
            0.498,
            0.242
          ],
          "content": "• Decline to place any bets at all."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.205,
            0.255,
            0.819,
            0.292
          ],
          "content": "• Bet on Belle. It costs \\$1 to place a bet; you will be paid \\$2 if she wins (for a net profit of \\$1)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.205,
            0.304,
            0.819,
            0.341
          ],
          "content": "• Bet on Jeb. It costs \\$1 to place a bet; you will be paid \\$11 if he wins (for a net profit of \\$10)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.353,
            0.819,
            0.391
          ],
          "content": "You believe that Belle has probability 0.7 of winning and that Jeb has probability 0.1 of winning."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.2,
            0.405,
            0.821,
            0.444
          ],
          "content": "1. Your goal is to maximize the expected value of your actions. What, if any, bet should you place, and what is your expected value?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.199,
            0.614,
            0.819,
            0.65
          ],
          "content": "2. Someone comes and offers you gambler’s anti-insurance. If you agree to it,"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.248,
            0.663,
            0.489,
            0.682
          ],
          "content": "• they play you \\$2 up front"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.247,
            0.688,
            0.597,
            0.707
          ],
          "content": "• you agree to pay 50% of any winnings."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.223,
            0.718,
            0.819,
            0.755
          ],
          "content": "How would it affect the expected value of each of your courses of action? What would be the best action to take now?"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.491,
            0.874,
            0.505,
            0.889
          ],
          "content": "3"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_idx": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.155,
            0.489,
            0.178
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "4 Still At The Races"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.194,
            0.818,
            0.23
          ],
          "content": "A shady character comes to offer you a free tip (a tip is a piece of information): he says Belle did not eat her breakfast. Assume that:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.205,
            0.243,
            0.818,
            0.279
          ],
          "content": "• The probability that a horse will win is dependent on the horse’s health and its speed."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.205,
            0.292,
            0.629,
            0.31
          ],
          "content": "• A horse’s health and its speed are independent."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.206,
            0.322,
            0.818,
            0.358
          ],
          "content": "• A healthy horse has a higher probability of eating breakfast than does a sick horse."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.206,
            0.371,
            0.71,
            0.39
          ],
          "content": "• Your informant is known to be accurate 80% of the time."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.402,
            0.819,
            0.476
          ],
          "content": "Draw a Bayesian network with 5 variables (T = you got this tip; $\\mathrm { ~ B ~ } { = }$ Belle ate her breakfast; H = Belle is healthy; W = Belle will win; F = Belle is fast). The relationships between the variables should reflect the problem description."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.657,
            0.818,
            0.695
          ],
          "content": "What is $P ( W )$ (in terms of values stored in the network’s conditional probability tables)?"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.491,
            0.875,
            0.505,
            0.888
          ],
          "content": "4"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_idx": 4,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.16,
            0.818,
            0.197
          ],
          "content": "What is $P ( W | T )$ (in terms of values stored in the network’s conditional probability tables)?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.178,
            0.406,
            0.305,
            0.43
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "5 Logic"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.444,
            0.818,
            0.499
          ],
          "content": "Write the following sentences in first-order logic, using $\\mathrm { S } ( \\mathbf { x } )$ for slow, $\\mathrm { S ( x , } \\mathrm { y ) }$ to mean that x is slower than $\\mathrm { y ,   H ( x ) }$ for horse $\\mathrm { B } ( \\mathbf { x } )$ for brown, and $\\mathrm { W ( x , } \\mathrm { r ) }$ for horse x winning race r."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.201,
            0.515,
            0.45,
            0.532
          ],
          "content": "1. All brown horses are slow."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.199,
            0.569,
            0.449,
            0.586
          ],
          "content": "2. All slow horses are brown."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.2,
            0.622,
            0.507,
            0.64
          ],
          "content": "3. All slow things are brown horses."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.2,
            0.676,
            0.469,
            0.692
          ],
          "content": "4. Some slow horses are brown."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.2,
            0.73,
            0.461,
            0.746
          ],
          "content": "5. The slowest horse is brown."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.199,
            0.784,
            0.496,
            0.8
          ],
          "content": "6. There is a winner in every race."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.492,
            0.874,
            0.505,
            0.888
          ],
          "content": "5"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_idx": 5,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.177,
            0.155,
            0.415,
            0.178
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "6 Clausal Form"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.194,
            0.807,
            0.212
          ],
          "content": "Given the following sentence in first-order logic, convert it to clausal form:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.416,
            0.229,
            0.577,
            0.248
          ],
          "content": "\\forall r. o (r) \\rightarrow \\exists x. w (x)",
          "image_source": "images/page_5_equation_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.178,
            0.382,
            0.305,
            0.406
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "7 Logic"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.419,
            0.488,
            0.576,
            0.508
          ],
          "content": "(\\exists x. P (x)) \\to P (b)",
          "image_source": "images/page_5_equation_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.416,
            0.522,
            0.579,
            0.542
          ],
          "content": "\\exists x. (P (x)) \\to P (b)\\left. \\right)",
          "image_source": "images/page_5_equation_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.42,
            0.819,
            0.474
          ],
          "content": "In a domain with only three objects, named by constants $$\\mathrm { a , ~ b , }$$ and $\\mathrm { c } ,$ give values for $P(a) ,  P(b)$ , and $\\mathrm { P ( c ) }$ that makes one of these sentences true and the other false."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.491,
            0.875,
            0.505,
            0.889
          ],
          "content": "6"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_idx": 6,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.155,
            0.624,
            0.18
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "8 Bayesian Network Structure"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.194,
            0.67,
            0.211
          ],
          "content": "Consider a Bayesian network with the following structure:"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.205,
            0.21,
            0.566,
            0.483
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.206,
                0.488,
                0.524,
                0.507
              ],
              "content": "Does computing P(M|A) depend on:"
            }
          ],
          "image_source": "images/page_6_image_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.206,
            0.517,
            0.302,
            0.537
          ],
          "content": "$P ( L | J ) ?$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.206,
            0.57,
            0.304,
            0.591
          ],
          "content": "$P ( K | I ) ?$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.206,
            0.624,
            0.308,
            0.645
          ],
          "content": "$P ( D | B ) ?$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.205,
            0.678,
            0.309,
            0.698
          ],
          "content": "$P ( H | G ) ?$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.733,
            0.82,
            0.788
          ],
          "content": "In the network above, if we decided not to include G in our network, but still wanted to model the joint distribution of all the other variables, what is the smallest network structure we could use?"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.492,
            0.874,
            0.505,
            0.888
          ],
          "content": "7"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_idx": 7,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.178,
            0.155,
            0.436,
            0.178
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "9 True and False"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.201,
            0.194,
            0.818,
            0.23
          ],
          "content": "1. It is sometimes worth paying for a test even if you will choose the same alternative no matter what the test results are."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.2,
            0.243,
            0.818,
            0.279
          ],
          "content": "2. A person for whom it is rational to play the lottery has a convex utility function."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.2,
            0.292,
            0.818,
            0.329
          ],
          "content": "3. If a dataset of dimension n is not linearly separable, it can be made linearly separable by adding extra features."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.201,
            0.341,
            0.818,
            0.378
          ],
          "content": "4. A network of perceptrons with step-function outputs induces a linear separator."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.201,
            0.39,
            0.601,
            0.408
          ],
          "content": "5. Inference in first-order logic is NP-complete."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.2,
            0.42,
            0.739,
            0.439
          ],
          "content": "6. A logical sentence is valid if it is true in some interpretation."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.201,
            0.452,
            0.818,
            0.487
          ],
          "content": "7. Iterative deepening search requires time exponential in the depth of the solution."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.201,
            0.5,
            0.82,
            0.536
          ],
          "content": "8. Iterative deepening search requires space exponential in the depth of the solution."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.492,
            0.874,
            0.505,
            0.888
          ],
          "content": "8"
        }
      ]
    }
